alex-d: quick poll: when we publish the JS SDK how should we handle fetch availability? options: require dev to install undici, ship undici as optionalDep, or rely purely on global fetch and document polyfills. thoughts?

lee-ops: my concern is end-user confusion. optionalDep is nice but npm will still fetch it unless using pnpm with hoisting rules. for edge-first customers we should avoid pulling undici into edge bundles.

nina-r: agree. conditional exports + optionalDependency feels right: runtime only uses undici if global fetch missing. also add a small helper that returns sync-or-null to support require() users.

pkg-bot: FYI we can use "exports" field for conditional entrypoints: {"import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs", "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs"} and still keep a light CJS shim.

sam-eng: another gotcha: TypeScript. If our types mention RequestInit from DOM lib, consumers without "lib": ["DOM"] will get errors. Do we want to ship our own minimal Fetch types under types/fetch.d.ts?\

alex-d: yep prefer minimal copy of types. keep it tiny: RequestInfo = string | URL | { toString(): string } etc. avoids forcing DOM lib. Document tradeoff clearly.

lee-ops: runtime differences: Bun/Node 20 support fetch and streaming differences; Cloudflare returns ReadableStream not Node streams. Our http-layer should accept either body types and normalize (readable stream, async iterator, Buffer). Add adapter utils.

nina-r: on bundlers: webpack config sometimes polyfills globalThis.fetch with node-fetch which breaks edge. We should add a short note: "do not polyfill fetch for edge bundles" and examples for Next.js and Vite.

sam-eng: sketch of runtime detection we can ship (ESM first):

```js
// fetch-adapter.mjs
export function getFetchSync() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  return null;
}

export async function ensureFetch() {
  const g = getFetchSync();
  if (g) return g;
  // dynamic import so bundlers don't include undici in edge builds
  const mod = await import('undici');
  return mod.fetch;
}
```

lee-ops: prefer this pattern. For CJS consumers we add a small require shim that exports getFetchSync and ensureFetch as functions that return Promises. avoids top-level await.

alex-d: packaging note: mark undici as optionalDependency and add peer or docs note that Node users should `npm i undici` on Node <20. Also list supported Node versions and behavior.

nina-r: tests: add CI matrix job that runs basic SDK calls under Node 16/18/20, Bun (latest), Deno (compat), and a Workers runtime (wrangler or miniflare). catch fetch semantics differences early.

sam-eng: about retries/auth: we should implement high-level retry wrapper independent of fetch impl so auth token refresh and idempotent retries are consistent. underlying pool options (undici agent) can be configured via client options but not required.

pkg-bot: remember to add exports for types: "./dist/types/index.d.ts" and keep types-only entry for TS tooling.

lee-ops: small ergonomics: include example in README showing how to opt into Node-only undici install: `npm i @redwood/inference undici` and a snippet for Next.js Edge vs Next.js Server. Many users confuse which runtime they target.

alex-d: action items:
- implement fetch-adapter + CJS shim (sam)
- add minimal fetch d.ts types and examples (nina)
- update package.json exports and optionalDependency (lee)
- CI matrix for runtimes (sam & nina)

sam-eng: will open PR with code + docs. tagging folks for review. thanks all.
